.\" Do not edit elm.1; edit _jnja/elm.1.j2. The COMMANDS section is built
.\" from _defs/commands.json, so it lists every command this build has.
.TH ELM 1 "{{ date }}" "elm {{ version }}" "User Commands"
.nh
.SH NAME
elm \- query the LogicMonitor REST API from the command line
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B elm
.RI [ "global options" ]
.I COMMAND
.RI [ "command options" ]
.br
.B elm \-\-list
.br
.B elm
.I COMMAND
.B \-\-info
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B elm
reads data from a LogicMonitor portal through its REST API (v3).
Each
.I COMMAND
is one API endpoint, generated from LogicMonitor's own API specification:
.B DeviceList
lists devices,
.B AlertList
alerts, and so on (see
.BR COMMANDS ).
Every request is a read: elm only has GET commands.
.PP
Requests are signed (HMAC\-SHA256) with the API token in a credentials
profile, so the token is never typed on the command line or sent anywhere
but the portal.
.PP
Options come in two places, and two letters mean different things in each:
.I global options
go before
.I COMMAND
and choose the profile and the output
.RB ( \-f
is the output format,
.B \-s
a proxy);
.I command options
go after it and shape the query
.RB ( \-f
is the fields,
.B \-s
the number of rows).
.SH GLOBAL OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-config " FILE"
Read the credentials from
.IR FILE ,
any .ini file in any directory.
.TP
.BI \-p ", " \-\-profile " NAME"
Use the profile
.IR ~/.config/logicmonitor/credentials/NAME.ini .
Comma\-separated names
.RI ( a,b )
run the command on each profile and add profile and account_name columns.
.TP
.BR \-l ", " \-\-list
List the profiles, mark the active one with *, show what each restricted
profile allows and denies, and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-ai
Print a quick\-start guide for AI assistants and exit.
.TP
.BI \-e ", " \-\-explode " FIELD"
One row per item of this list field, its keys as
.I FIELD.key
columns. Repeat only for a list inside it
.RB ( "\-e a \-e a.b" ).
.TP
.BR \-t ", " \-\-localtime
Show epoch times in the results as local times.
.TP
.BR \-d ", " \-\-diff
With several profiles: show only the rows that are not the same on all of them.
.TP
.BI \-i ", " \-\-access_id " ID"
The API token's access id, instead of the profile's.
.TP
.BI \-k ", " \-\-access_key " KEY"
The API token's access key, instead of the profile's. It shows in the
process list and shell history: prefer a profile.
.TP
.BI \-a ", " \-\-account_name " NAME"
The portal's account name, as in NAME.logicmonitor.com.
.TP
.BI \-s ", " \-\-proxy " HOST PORT"
Send requests through a SOCKS5 proxy.
.TP
.BI \-f ", " \-\-format " FORMAT"
Output format (default json): csv, tsv, json, jsonl, prettyjson, xml,
prettyxml, html, prettyhtml, gfm, pipe, jira, latex, md, rst, tab, values,
raw, txt, sqlite, and api, curl and wget.
The last three print the request URL or a command that repeats it,
.B including the signed Authorization header:
do not share their output.
.TP
.BR \-H ", " \-\-noheader
Hide the column headers.
.TP
.BR \-I ", " \-\-index
Show the row indices.
.TP
.BI \-o ", " \-\-filename " FILE"
Write the output to
.I FILE
instead of standard output.
.TP
.BI \-\-head " TEXT"
Text to print before the output.
.TP
.BI \-\-foot " TEXT"
Text to print after the output.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-verbose
More detail on standard error:
.B \-v
for INFO,
.B \-vv
for DEBUG.
.TP
.B \-\-halt\-on\-api\-error
Stop at the first API error instead of carrying on.
.TP
.BI \-\-cacert " FILE"
Verify TLS with this CA bundle, for a proxy that inspects TLS.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Show the version and exit.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Show the help and exit. After a restricted profile it lists only the
commands that profile allows.
.SH COMMAND OPTIONS
Each command has its own; these are the common ones.
.B elm
.I COMMAND
.B \-\-help
lists them all, and
.B \-\-info
the fields the command returns, with verified notes and examples.
.TP
.BI \-f ", " \-\-fields " FIELD,..."
Only include the listed fields.
.TP
.BI \-F ", " \-\-filter " FIELD OP VALUE,..."
Filter the rows. Operators:
.B :
equals,
.B !:
does not equal,
.B ~
includes,
.B !~
does not include,
.BR > ", " < ", " >: ", " <:
compare.
Separate filters with commas; escape a comma in a value with a backslash.
Quote the whole filter for the shell:
.RB \(aq hostStatus~dead \(aq.
.TP
.BI \-s ", " \-\-size " N"
How many rows (default 50, at most 1000);
.B 0
asks for all of them, up to 1000. Page past that with
.BR \-o .
.TP
.BI \-o ", " \-\-offset " N"
Start from this row.
.TP
.BI \-S ", " \-\-sort " [+|\-]FIELD,..."
Sort by field, ascending (+) or descending (\-).
.TP
.BR \-c ", " \-\-count
Count the rows this query returned.
.TP
.BR \-C ", " \-\-total
Count every row matching
.BR \-F ,
as LogicMonitor reports it, without fetching them.
.TP
.B \-\-info
Show the fields, notes and example commands, then exit. Needs no credentials.
.PP
Commands for one object take its id as an option, for example
.BR "elm DeviceById \-\-id 42" .
.SH PROFILES
A profile is an .ini file in
.I ~/.config/logicmonitor/credentials/
holding
.BR access_id ,
.B access_key
and
.BR account_name .
.B config.ini
is the default; keep it pointed at the safest portal, and give the others
names you must choose with
.BR \-p .
.PP
A profile can be limited to some commands:
.PP
.RS
.nf
allowed_commands = [\(aqDeviceList\(aq, \(aqAlert*\(aq]
denied_commands = [\(aqAdmin*\(aq, \(aqApiToken*\(aq]
.fi
.RE
.PP
Patterns are shell\-style and case\-sensitive, and each list must stay on
one line. Any other command stops before a request is sent, with exit
status 3;
.B denied_commands
wins over
.BR allowed_commands .
This is a guard rail, not a security boundary: what limits access is the
role of the API token in LogicMonitor. The repository's
.B ai.example.ini
is a restricted profile for AI assistants, with the reasons for each choice.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
An error: bad credentials, an unreadable profile, a failed request.
.TP
.B 2
A usage error; or with
.BR \-d ,
a comparison that could not be made or did not see every row.
.TP
.B 3
The profile does not allow the command (or denies it). No request was sent.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B ELM_RELAX_X509_STRICT
Set to 1 to accept a TLS\-inspecting proxy's CA that Python 3.13's strict
certificate checks reject. The chain and hostname are still verified.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/logicmonitor/credentials/config.ini
The default profile.
.TP
.I ~/.config/logicmonitor/credentials/NAME.ini
The profile
.BR "\-p NAME" .
Files ending in .example.ini are skipped by
.BR \-\-list .
.SH EXAMPLES
List the profiles:
.PP
.RS
.nf
.B elm \-\-list
.fi
.RE
.PP
The devices that are down, three columns, as CSV:
.PP
.RS
.nf
.B elm \-f csv DeviceList \-s 0 \-F \(aqhostStatus~dead\(aq \e
.B "    \-f id,displayName,hostStatus"
.fi
.RE
.PP
How many open critical alerts, on the profile prod:
.PP
.RS
.nf
.B elm \-p prod AlertList \-F \(aqcleared:false,severity:4\(aq \-C
.fi
.RE
.PP
The same query on two portals, showing only what differs:
.PP
.RS
.nf
.B elm \-p portal01,portal02 \-d CollectorList \-f description,build
.fi
.RE
.PP
What a command returns, without running it:
.PP
.RS
.nf
.B elm DeviceList \-\-info
.fi
.RE
